A provider established in a third country must appoint an **authorised representative** established in the Union by written mandate, before making its high-risk AI system available on the Union market (Article 22(1)). Under Article 22(3), the mandate must empower the representative to:
- verify that the EU declaration of conformity and the technical documentation have been drawn up and that conformity assessment has been carried out;
- keep the provider's contact details, the declaration, the technical documentation and any notified-body certificate available to the competent authorities for 10 years;
- supply information, documentation and logs when authorities make a reasoned request;
- cooperate with the authorities and comply with the registration obligations;
- act as the authorities' contact on all compliance issues.

Article 22(4) requires the representative to end the mandate if the provider acts contrary to its obligations, and then immediately inform the market surveillance authority.

**Provider obligations under Article 16 are about making a high-risk AI system compliant before it reaches the market.** **Deployer obligations under Article 26 are about how that system is then used.** The provider must:
- ensure the system meets the Section 2 requirements;
- indicate its name and contact address;
- operate a quality management system;
- keep the documentation and the logs;
- have the system undergo conformity assessment;
- draw up the EU declaration of conformity;
- affix the CE marking;
- register the system.

The deployer must:
- use the system in accordance with the instructions for use;
- assign human oversight to natural persons with the necessary competence, training and authority;
- monitor operation and inform the provider of serious incidents;
- keep the logs under its control for at least six months;
- inform workers' representatives and affected workers before workplace use;
- comply with registration where it is a public authority.

Article 16 sets out the provider's obligations for a high-risk AI system used for utility planning. Under Article 16(1), the provider must:
- ensure the system meets the Section 2 requirements;
- show its name and contact address on the system;
- operate a quality management system;
- keep the documentation and, when under its control, the automatically generated logs;
- ensure the system undergoes conformity assessment before it is placed on the market;
- draw up an EU declaration of conformity;
- affix the CE marking;
- register the system.

Article 8 requires the system to meet the Section 2 requirements, taking into account its intended purpose and the generally acknowledged state of the art. These requirements are risk management, data governance, technical documentation, record-keeping, transparency, human oversight, and accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity.

**An EU establishment is not needed, but if the system is high-risk the provider must appoint an authorised representative established in the Union before making it available in Germany (Article 22(1)).** The appointment must be made by written mandate, and Article 22(2) requires the provider to enable the representative to carry out its tasks. Under Article 22(3), the authorised representative must:
- verify that the EU declaration of conformity and technical documentation exist and that a conformity assessment was carried out;
- keep these documents, the provider's contact details and any notified-body certificate available to the authorities for 10 years;
- give documentation and log access to a competent authority on reasoned request;
- cooperate in risk-mitigation actions;
- handle or check the EU database registration.

Article 22(4) requires the representative to end the mandate, and tell the market surveillance authority, if the provider breaches the Regulation. If the system is limited risk, no authorised representative is required, and the provider's duty is the transparency obligation in Article 50. That means telling natural persons they are interacting with an AI system and marking synthetic content in a machine-readable format. Whatever the risk tier, Article 4 requires a sufficient level of AI literacy among staff.

**Article 51(2)** sets one cumulative compute threshold. A general-purpose AI model is presumed to have high impact capabilities when the cumulative computation used for its training exceeds **10^25 FLOPs**.

**Article 51(1)** classifies a model as a general-purpose AI model with **systemic risk** in two cases. Either it has high impact capabilities, or the Commission designates it as having equivalent capabilities or impact.

**Article 51(3)** lets the Commission amend this threshold by delegated acts.

On top of the baseline duties for general-purpose AI models, **Article 55(1)** requires providers of such models to:
- carry out model evaluation, including adversarial testing;
- assess and mitigate systemic risks at Union level;
- report serious incidents to the AI Office;
- ensure adequate cybersecurity protection for the model and its physical infrastructure.

**Providers** of high-risk AI systems have the design-side duties under Article 16:
- meet the Section 2 requirements;
- put their name and contact address on the system;
- operate a quality management system;
- keep the documentation, and the logs under their control;
- complete conformity assessment before placing the system on the market;
- draw up the EU declaration of conformity;
- affix the CE marking;
- register the system;
- take corrective action.

**Deployers** have the use-side duties under Article 26:
- use the system according to its instructions for use;
- give human oversight to natural persons with the necessary competence, training and authority;
- monitor how the system operates and tell the provider about serious incidents;
- keep the logs under their control for at least six months.

Employers must also inform workers' representatives and affected workers before using the system in the workplace. Deployers that are public authorities must also register.

**Article 9 requires a risk management system for high-risk AI systems that is established, implemented, documented and maintained (Article 9(1)).** It must run as a continuous iterative process over the whole lifecycle, with regular systematic review (Article 9(2)). That process must cover four steps:

- identifying and analysing the known and reasonably foreseeable risks to health, safety or fundamental rights;
- estimating and evaluating risks under the intended purpose and under reasonably foreseeable misuse;
- evaluating other risks found in post-market monitoring data;
- adopting targeted risk management measures.

It covers only risks that can reasonably be mitigated or eliminated through development, design or technical information (Article 9(3)).

**Weighing and residual risk.** The measures must take into account the combined effects of the requirements (Article 9(4)). They must also make residual risk acceptable through elimination by design, mitigation and control measures, and information and training for deployers (Article 9(5)).

**Testing.** Testing must identify the right measures and confirm consistent performance (Article 9(6)). It may take place in real world conditions (Article 9(7)). It must happen before the system is placed on the market, against prior defined metrics and probabilistic thresholds (Article 9(8)).

**Other duties.** Providers must consider the impact on persons under 18 and other vulnerable groups (Article 9(9)). They may combine these procedures with risk management procedures required under other Union law (Article 9(10)).
